      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.35.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g6.35.6', 'g6.35.8', 'g6.35.10'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“I am the bread of life”",
          body: "This is the first of the seven great **“I am”** sayings that carry the Gospel — *I am the bread, the light, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection, the way, the vine.* Each takes something ordinary and life-sustaining and says: the reality behind it is me. Here the picture is hunger, the most basic of human needs. The crowd wants bread that fills the stomach for a day; Jesus offers himself as the bread that satisfies the deep hunger of a life — *“whoever comes to me will never hunger.”* Notice the two verbs he lays side by side: **coming** to him and **believing** in him are the same act, the “eating” this whole chapter is about. And notice the scope of the promise: *never* hunger, *never* thirst. It is not that the believer feels no more longing, but that the longing has found its end — the manna kept Israel alive one more day and left them craving; this bread gives a life that does not run out (v. 27). Everything the loaves on the hillside pointed to is standing here, saying *take, and eat.*",
        },
      ],
