      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.53.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g6.53.14', 'g6.53.23', 'g6.53.26'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "Eat the flesh, drink the blood",
          body: "No sentence in the Gospel is harder or more deliberately shocking. To a Jewish crowd forbidden even to eat meat with the blood in it (Lev 17), Jesus says life comes *only* by **eating his flesh and drinking his blood** — and he will not soften it as they recoil. What does it mean? Not cannibalism, and not, at this moment, a lecture on a rite that did not yet exist. **Verse 63 is the key he gives**: *“the Spirit gives life; the flesh is of no help… the words I have spoken to you are spirit and life.”* To “eat” and “drink” is the same act the whole chapter has been circling — to **come** and to **believe** (v. 35) — but now pressed to its most personal and total: not merely to admire Jesus, or to accept his ideas, but to take his crucified self — flesh given, blood poured out — *into* you, until his life becomes your life (“abides in me, and I in him,” v. 56). It is faith in the mode of *feeding*: utter dependence, daily, from the inside. Christians ever since have rightly heard the Lord’s Supper here — the bread and cup are exactly this truth made touchable — but the eating Jesus commands is wider and deeper than any meal: it is the receiving of a given-up life by everyone who would live. The manna kept bodies alive a day; this food raises the whole person *on the last day* (v. 54).",
        },
      ],
