      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.68.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g6.68.7', 'g6.68.8', 'g6.68.9'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“Lord, to whom shall we go?”",
          body: "It is not a burst of confidence so much as a quiet, clear-eyed realism — and it is the truest thing anyone says in the chapter. Peter does not claim the hard saying is now easy; he simply sees there is nowhere else to go. Once you have met the one who **has the words of eternal life**, every alternative is exposed as a road back to death. Faith is often exactly this: not that all the difficulty has dissolved, but that leaving has become unthinkable — *where else would we turn?* And Peter goes further, to confession: **“we have believed, and have come to know, that you are the Holy One of God”** (v. 69) — belief ripening into knowledge, staying when others leave. It stands as the deliberate counterpoint to the crowd: the sign fed thousands and lost them; the *word* keeps the few who have tasted that it is life. (John lets the shadow fall at once, v. 70: even among the Twelve, chosen and staying, one will betray — faith’s company is never yet pure this side of the end.)",
        },
      ],
